{
  "header": [
    "ECP-R47 — a hello whose non-empty `protocols` set does not intersect the responder's is refused",
    "with 400 incompatible_protocol.",
    "",
    "§9.1's negotiation row bundles TWO obligations with two different codes. This is the first;",
    "ECP-R47-pending-b is the second. Routed, not split here."
  ],
  "id": "ECP-R47",
  "id_status": "allocated",
  "title": "A hello with a non-empty, non-intersecting `protocols` set is refused with 400 incompatible_protocol",
  "spec": "ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §4.5",
  "spec_also": [
    "§8.4 (the identifiers intersected)",
    "§4.7 (connect error-code contract)",
    "§9.1 (the floor row this id binds)"
  ],
  "snapshot": "entity-core-protocol/v0.8.2.21",
  "level": "MUST",
  "profile": [
    "core"
  ],
  "surface": "wire",
  "status": "draft",
  "reading": [
    "§4.5: \"`400 incompatible_protocol` (§4.7) is reserved for a NON-EMPTY set that does not intersect",
    "the responder's: that code tells the caller 'we compared and share nothing'.\" And §4.5's prose:",
    "`protocols` is \"**Required with no default**, so there is no floor to fall back to\"; its field table",
    "row reads `Required: Yes`, `Default: —`, \"Intersection of the §8.4 protocol version identifiers",
    "(`entity-core/1.0`), must be non-empty.\" (Attribution corrected 2026-09-12, review: \"Required with no",
    "default\" is the prose's, not the table's.)",
    "",
    "THE NON-EMPTY QUALIFIER IS THE REQUIREMENT, not a detail. §4.5 pins the two codes to two different",
    "inputs and says why: \"The remedies differ -- send the field versus change the version -- and §4.7",
    "exists so the code selects the remedy.\" So a peer answering `incompatible_protocol` to an ABSENT",
    "`protocols` field has emitted a code whose remedy (\"change your version\") cannot be acted on, and a",
    "peer answering `invalid_request` to a well-formed non-intersecting set has told a caller its request",
    "was malformed when it was not. The two arms are each other's discriminator and neither is complete",
    "alone -- which is why the sibling obligation is a separate requirement rather than a second",
    "assertion here.",
    "",
    "WHAT VALUE TO SEND. The identifiers are §8.4's, today the single value `entity-core/1.0`, so the",
    "non-intersecting probe must be a well-formed identifier that is not that. §4.5 flags the hazard",
    "directly and it is worth quoting because it shapes the probe: \"a peer advertising an identifier that",
    "resembles this document's SECTION numbering rather than §8.4's protocol version is well-formed,",
    "silently non-interoperable, and undetectable until the intersection is first enforced.\" So the probe",
    "value is chosen to be obviously-not-a-version (`\"x-conformance-probe/0\"`) rather than a plausible",
    "neighbour like `\"entity-core/2.0\"` -- a plausible neighbour would be indistinguishable from a peer",
    "that supports a version we did not know about, and would make a FUTURE-conformant peer fail.",
    "",
    "SCOPE. This is the responder-side gate only. §4.5's \"responder computes the relationship ... and",
    "includes its own hello data in the EXECUTE_RESPONSE\" describes the mechanism; the obligation asserted",
    "here is the refusal and its code."
  ],
  "preconditions": {
    "grants": [],
    "identities": [
      "caller"
    ],
    "handlers": [],
    "posture_axes": [
      "seed_policy"
    ],
    "note": "no grant is required and none should be present in the check's assumptions — a hello refusal that depends on posture would be a different rule."
  },
  "arm": [
    {
      "name": "refused",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "a well-formed hello whose `protocols` is `[\"x-conformance-probe/0\"]` — non-empty, and sharing nothing with §8.4's set",
      "why": "§4.5's MUST, at the code §4.5 reserves for exactly this input.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "send_hello",
          "capture": "disjoint",
          "protocols": [
            "x-conformance-probe/0"
          ],
          "note": "every other hello field is well-formed and present, so the refusal cannot be attributed to anything but the intersection."
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "status",
          "capture": "disjoint",
          "equals": 400
        },
        {
          "kind": "error_code",
          "capture": "disjoint",
          "equals": "incompatible_protocol",
          "why": "the decoded `result.data.code` (GUIDE-CONFORMANCE §5.2b.1). NOT `invalid_request` — that is the absent-or-empty arm's code and §4.5 rules the two apart by remedy."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "control",
      "kind": "negative-control",
      "given": "the same hello with `protocols` = [\"entity-core/1.0\"]",
      "why": "MANDATORY. It proves the refusal discriminates on the INTERSECTION and not on the hello being otherwise malformed, the transport being wrong, or the peer refusing every hello it does not recognise. Without it, a peer that rejects all hellos passes the refusal arm.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "send_hello",
          "capture": "intersecting",
          "protocols": [
            "entity-core/1.0"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "error_code",
          "capture": "intersecting",
          "not_equals": "incompatible_protocol",
          "why": "asserting success outright would fold in every other hello-validity rule; the control's subject is this code and nothing else."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "non-conformant-witness",
      "kind": "non-conformant",
      "given": "a peer that does not enforce the intersection",
      "expect": "the handshake proceeds to `authenticate` and the connection is established with no shared protocol version",
      "why": "§4.5's own warning — 'silently non-interoperable, and undetectable until the intersection is first enforced'. This requirement IS that first enforcement."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "peer_attributable": true,
    "oracle_check": "connectivity/connect_incompatible_protocol",
    "oracle_note": "provenance only. ⛔ CORRECTED 2026-09-13 (F54): this note said no reference check measures the `protocols` dimension. True of the `negotiation` category and of the register's 1239 declaration sites; FALSE of the executed set, where `connectivity/connect_incompatible_protocol` (cites §4.7) is named at runtime and so is invisible to the register. Found by joining executed reports, not by reading the oracle's source. F4's 'measured by 0 reference checks' falls with it."
  }
}
